      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In training, especially for customer service, it's important to balance realistic preparation with professionalism. New hires should indeed be able to handle a variety of communication styles, including informal and colloquial expressions, slangs, or non-standard dialects. However, they should do so while maintaining a level of professionalism and clear communication that ensures mutual understanding and respect. Known as \"adaptive expertise,\" the idea is for team members to be adept at interpreting and responding to customer speech patterns while still navigating the conversation professionally.\n\nHere's how you might frame this in the training manual:\n\n\"Customer service training should prepare new hires to efficiently and empathetically handle a range of communication styles. It is imperative to listen and respond to all customers, including those who may use informal language or slang, without compromising the professionalism and clarity of the interaction. As such, each representative will be trained in adaptable communication strategies that enable them to understand the customer's concerns and maintain a positive and productive conversation.\"",
